                    'extra' => ['category' => 'adjectives', 'cefr' => 'Beginner'],
                ])->all(),
            ],
        ];
    }

    /**
     * Indonesian folk tales retold in simple English — familiar stories make
     * reading in a new language less intimidating, since the plot is already
     * known. `content` is plain text (paragraphs separated by a blank line,
     * not HTML) because the reading player splits on blank lines; `items`
     * carry the glossary (difficult word → Indonesian meaning).
     */
    private function readingStories(): array
    {
        return [
            [
                'title' => 'Malin Kundang',
                'level' => 'Intermediate',
                'summary' => 'Cerita rakyat Sumatera Barat tentang anak durhaka yang dikutuk menjadi batu.',
                'minutes' => 12,
                'xp' => 25,
                'content' => "Long ago, in a small village by the sea in West Sumatra, there lived a poor woman and her son, Malin Kundang. Malin's father had died when he was a baby, so his mother worked very hard, fishing and selling what she caught, just to feed her only child.\n\nMalin grew into a strong, clever young man. One day, he told his mother he wanted to sail to a faraway city to find a better life. His mother did not want him to go, but Malin insisted. \"I will come back rich, Mother,\" he promised. \"I will make our lives better.\" With a heavy heart, she let him board a large ship and sail away.\n\nYears passed, and Malin did not return. He worked hard in the city, and slowly he became successful. He married a beautiful, wealthy woman and became a rich merchant with his own ship. But Malin never once wrote a letter to his mother.\n\nOne day, Malin's ship came to a port near his home village. His old mother heard that a rich merchant had arrived, and she rushed to the harbor, hoping it was her son. When she saw Malin standing on the deck in fine clothes, she recognized him immediately and ran toward him, calling his name with joy.\n\nBut Malin was ashamed. He did not want his elegant wife and his crew to know that this poor, ragged old woman was his mother. \"Get away from me, old woman!\" he shouted. \"I have never had a mother like you!\" He pushed her away and ordered his men to sail off.\n\nHeartbroken, Malin's mother fell to her knees on the sand. Tears filled her eyes, and anger filled her heart. She raised her hands to the sky and prayed. \"If he is truly not my son, let him leave safely. But if he is, oh God, punish him for his cruelty and pride.\"\n\nNo sooner had she finished praying than the sky turned dark. Thunder roared, and a terrible storm struck Malin's ship. Waves crashed over the deck, tearing the sails apart. Malin realized his mistake too late. \"Forgive me, Mother!\" he cried out into the storm, but it was too late.\n\nThe ship was destroyed, and Malin's body slowly turned to stone, right there on the beach. To this day, people say you can still see a rock shaped like a broken ship and a kneeling man on the coast of West Sumatra — a reminder of what happens to a child who forgets his mother's love.",
                'translated_content' => "Dahulu kala, di sebuah desa kecil di tepi laut Sumatera Barat, hiduplah seorang perempuan miskin bersama anaknya, Malin Kundang. Ayah Malin telah meninggal sejak ia masih bayi, sehingga ibunya bekerja sangat keras, menangkap ikan dan menjualnya, hanya untuk menghidupi anak semata wayangnya.\n\nMalin tumbuh menjadi pemuda yang kuat dan cerdas. Suatu hari, ia berkata kepada ibunya bahwa ia ingin berlayar ke kota yang jauh untuk mencari kehidupan yang lebih baik. Ibunya tidak ingin ia pergi, tetapi Malin bersikeras. \"Aku akan pulang dengan kaya, Ibu,\" janjinya. \"Aku akan membuat hidup kita lebih baik.\" Dengan hati berat, sang ibu mengizinkannya naik ke sebuah kapal besar dan berlayar pergi.\n\nBertahun-tahun berlalu, dan Malin tak kunjung kembali. Ia bekerja keras di kota, dan perlahan-lahan menjadi sukses. Ia menikahi seorang perempuan cantik dan kaya, lalu menjadi saudagar kaya dengan kapal miliknya sendiri. Namun, Malin tidak pernah sekali pun menulis surat kepada ibunya.\n\nSuatu hari, kapal Malin merapat di sebuah pelabuhan dekat desa asalnya. Ibunya yang sudah tua mendengar bahwa seorang saudagar kaya telah tiba, dan ia bergegas ke pelabuhan, berharap itu adalah anaknya. Ketika melihat Malin berdiri di geladak dengan pakaian bagus, ia langsung mengenalinya dan berlari ke arahnya sambil memanggil namanya dengan gembira.\n\nNamun Malin merasa malu. Ia tidak ingin istrinya yang anggun dan para awak kapalnya tahu bahwa perempuan tua yang miskin dan compang-camping itu adalah ibunya. \"Pergi dariku, perempuan tua!\" teriaknya. \"Aku tidak pernah punya ibu sepertimu!\" Ia mendorong ibunya dan memerintahkan anak buahnya untuk berlayar pergi.\n\nDengan hati yang hancur, ibu Malin jatuh berlutut di atas pasir. Air mata memenuhi matanya, dan amarah memenuhi hatinya. Ia mengangkat tangannya ke langit dan berdoa. \"Jika ia benar-benar bukan anakku, biarkan ia pergi dengan selamat. Tetapi jika ia memang anakku, ya Tuhan, hukumlah ia atas kekejaman dan kesombongannya.\"\n\nBelum lama ia selesai berdoa, langit tiba-tiba menjadi gelap. Guntur menggelegar, dan badai dahsyat menghantam kapal Malin. Ombak menerjang geladak, merobek layar-layarnya. Malin menyadari kesalahannya, tetapi sudah terlambat. \"Maafkan aku, Ibu!\" teriaknya di tengah badai, namun semuanya sudah terlambat.\n\nKapal itu hancur, dan tubuh Malin perlahan-lahan berubah menjadi batu, tepat di pantai itu. Hingga kini, orang-orang berkata bahwa kita masih bisa melihat batu berbentuk kapal yang hancur dan seorang pria yang berlutut di pesisir Sumatera Barat — sebuah pengingat akan apa yang terjadi pada anak yang melupakan kasih sayang ibunya.",
                'items' => [
                    ['term' => 'insisted', 'reading' => null, 'romaji' => null, 'meaning' => 'bersikeras', 'example' => null, 'example_meaning' => null, 'extra' => ['type' => 'glossary']],
